    "message": {
      "content": "# Distributed Tensor and Expert Parallel Inference: A Technical Note\n\n## 1. Overview and Motivation\n\nModern large models exceed the memory and compute capacity of a single accelerator. Two complementary parallelism strategies address this during inference:\n\n- **Tensor parallelism (TP)**: Splits individual weight matrices (and activations) across devices. Every device holds a shard of every layer and processes the *same* token sequence simultaneously.\n- **Expert parallelism (EP)**: Splits the *experts* of a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) layer across devices. Each device holds a subset of experts; different tokens are routed to different devices.\n\nIn practice, large MoE models (e.g., DeepSeek-V3, Mixtral-class architectures, Switch-style transformers) combine both: attention and dense layers use TP, while the MoE feed-forward blocks use EP. The combination is sometimes called **TP+EP hybrid parallelism**.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tensor Parallel Inference\n\n### 2.1 Sharding scheme\n\nConsider a transformer feed-forward block:\n\n```\nY = W2 \u00b7 GeLU(W1 \u00b7 x)\n```\n\nwith `W1 \u2208 R^{h\u00d74h}`, `W2 \u2208 R^{4h\u00d7h}`, and `P` devices.\n\n- **Column-parallel (W1)**: Each device holds `W1_i \u2208 R^{h\u00d7(4h/P)}`. Input `x` is replicated; each device computes a slice of the intermediate activation: `a_i = GeLU(W1_i \u00b7 x) \u2208 R^{4h/P}`.\n- **Row-parallel (W2)**: Each device holds `W2_i \u2208 R^{(4h/P)\u00d7h}`. Each device multiplies its *own* slice `a_i` by `W2_i`, producing a partial sum `y_i = W2_i \u00b7 a_i \u2208 R^h`.\n\nBecause GeLU is",
